virglrenderer: Venus memory counts against the guest GPU memory budget

What: Venus (Vulkan) device memory and Venus shared-memory blobs are charged
against the same budget as the GL resources (virgl-resource-memory-budget.patch,
OMACVM_GPU_MEMORY_MB, default a quarter of the Mac's memory): one budget per VM.
vkAllocateMemory past it fails with VK_ERROR_OUT_OF_DEVICE_MEMORY, a shm blob
past it is not made; the QEMU log says so at the 1st, 2nd, 4th ... refusal.
Each allocation is charged in whole pages.
The charge goes with the storage, not with one object: every object that keeps
the storage alive holds a reference (struct virgl_gpu_charge): the
VkDeviceMemory, memory imported from it, the Venus context's resource, and the
virgl_resource of the guest's blob (handed over in the CREATE_RESOURCE reply,
like the Metal heap pointer). The bytes come back when the last one is gone.
So a guest that exports a blob, frees the memory and closes its GEM handle
(the kernel then sends DETACH) but keeps the dma-buf fd or a mapping still
pays for the storage until the blob itself is freed. Memory imported from a
resource this context did not make (no charge known here) is charged again at
the resource's size: counted twice rather than not at all.
The budget counter is atomic (Venus allocates on its ring threads) and is read
once per process (virgl_gpu_budget_init, called by vrend and vkr).
Why: Venus device memory was not limited at all: a guest Vulkan app could take
the Mac's memory (standards section 4).
Limits: the blob's reference only crosses to the virgl_resource when the render
server runs in QEMU's process (OmacVM on macOS:
virgl-darwin-venus-in-process.patch). A forked render server (Linux) keeps its
own count per process and its charge ends with the context's resource. Venus
resources only the guest's kernel sees (guest memory blobs) are not host memory
and are not charged.
Upstream: not sent.
What the app sees: guest Mesa venus allocates asynchronously by default, so
vkAllocateMemory returns VK_SUCCESS in the guest; the app's next use of that
memory finds no object, the context is lost and the guest's Mesa ends the app
(like any Venus loss, virgl-context-loss-report.patch). With
VN_PERF=no_async_mem_alloc in the guest the app gets VK_ERROR_OUT_OF_DEVICE_MEMORY
and can go on.
Tests: Tests/virgl/test-venus-budget-storage.c (build time): a blob's charge stays
after DETACH and goes with the blob; fails without this patch.
Tests/virgl/test-resource-budget.c still passes (the GL side uses the shared
counter). Venus in a test VM (gpu-2.9.0 runtime with this patch,
OMACVM_GPU_MEMORY_MB=2048, tests/graphics/guest/vk-alloc.c, 256 MB blocks):
synchronous, the 7th block (1902 MB in use with the desktop) gets
VK_ERROR_OUT_OF_DEVICE_MEMORY and, after freeing, allocations work again; async,
the app ends at vkFreeMemory and the VM, desktop and the next Vulkan app go on.
Without the patch all 24 blocks (6 GB) are made. vk-alloc keepfd (make
exportable, export the dma-buf fd, free, keep the fd; 256 MB blocks, same VM):
with the first version of this patch all 24 blocks (6 GB) were kept past the
2 GB budget; now the 7th is refused (1902 MB in use), and after the app ends
the bytes come back. Guest Mesa (main) then crashes in vn_AllocateMemory: its
memory report reads mem->base_bo of the failed export allocation (a guest
Mesa bug in its error path, not fixed here).
